WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.563
A mouse olfactory neuron carries about a thousand receptor genes, in each of its two

2
00:00:08.563 --> 00:00:12.023
genome copies. It uses one copy of one gene.

3
00:00:14.217 --> 00:00:19.817
In 1994, Chess and colleagues, in Richard Axel's laboratory, made the puzzle sharp.

4
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:24.967
A neuron's receptor transcript comes from just one of its two alleles.

5
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:28.647
So the neuron faces about two thousand candidates.

6
00:00:29.507 --> 00:00:32.147
A mature neuron uses one, and keeps using it.

7
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.770
This is called singular choice.

8
00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:39.070
One gene, from one allele, in nearly every mature mouse neuron.

9
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.740
It describes where development ends, not every stage on the way.

10
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:45.960
Picture three broad phases.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.620
In early progenitors, the receptor genes are silenced, and little or no receptor RNA

12
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.620
is detected.

13
00:00:54.483 --> 00:00:59.143
From late precursors into immature neurons, several receptors are transcribed

14
00:00:59.143 --> 00:01:03.243
weakly. Single-cell data infer competing enhancer hubs.

15
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:09.433
In mature neurons, one gene from one allele is expressed, and feedback helps hold

16
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:10.433
the choice.

17
00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:15.917
Why is this hard? Suppose each gene had a small chance of escaping silence,

18
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:17.977
independent of every other gene.

19
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:24.490
And suppose nothing afterwards shuts off extra genes or removes any neurons, and

20
00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.690
every neuron is counted at the same time.

21
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:31.070
Then the number of escapes per neuron is close to a Poisson distribution.

22
00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:34.093
You can set the average to anything.

23
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.973
What is the largest fraction of neurons that can have exactly one receptor?

24
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.033
In this model, about 37 percent at most.

25
00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:52.313
The exactly-one bar peaks at an average of one escape per neuron.

26
00:01:55.916 --> 00:01:59.216
Make escapes much rarer, and most neurons express nothing.

27
00:02:00.136 --> 00:02:02.916
Make them much more common, and most express two or more.

28
00:02:03.866 --> 00:02:07.586
Yet mature mouse neurons nearly all express exactly one.

29
00:02:08.366 --> 00:02:11.266
So this simple, unregulated model is insufficient.

30
00:02:13.416 --> 00:02:18.896
Mouse experiments support competition and receptor-dependent stabilization, but the

31
00:02:18.896 --> 00:02:21.676
arithmetic alone does not identify the mechanism.

32
00:02:22.766 --> 00:02:24.926
Here is the one idea for this lesson.

33
00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:29.006
Silence every gene, let a few compete, and lock in the winner.

34
00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:33.276
Repressive chromatin covers the receptor genes first.

35
00:02:34.176 --> 00:02:37.276
A few are transcribed weakly and compete at enhancer hubs.

36
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.080
The leading gene's RNA helps quiet its rivals.

37
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.900
Then its receptor protein sets off a stress signal.

38
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:51.863
That signal turns down Lsd1, an enzyme that new receptor expression depends on.

39
00:02:52.663 --> 00:02:54.043
Further escapes become rare.

40
00:02:55.016 --> 00:02:57.796
Picture a library where every book is chained shut.

41
00:02:58.616 --> 00:03:00.916
A key frees a few, and they compete to be read.

42
00:03:01.883 --> 00:03:03.983
The loudest reader drowns out the others.

43
00:03:04.823 --> 00:03:08.583
Then a bell rings and the key is taken away, so new books rarely open.

44
00:03:09.583 --> 00:03:12.223
In the mouse, the chains are repressive chromatin.

45
00:03:13.003 --> 00:03:15.623
The key is a step that depends on Lsd1.

46
00:03:16.423 --> 00:03:17.803
The bell is a stress pathway.

47
00:03:18.783 --> 00:03:23.523
The chapters ahead follow each step: the silence, the competing hubs, and the lock.
